Note on #1 (2026-09-24-sello-how-do-we-know.md):

Correction, prompted by a reviewer on Reddit. Two sentences in this post claim more than sello proves. 'Not one word has changed since': a seal proves the canonical text is unchanged (Unicode NFC, LF line endings, trailing spaces removed), not that the file is identical byte for byte. No word can change and still pass, but spaces, line endings and Unicode composition can. 'So a record can't be quietly backdated': an OpenTimestamps anchor proves the log existed no later than the anchoring block, so nothing can be rewritten or inserted before an anchor without showing. It does not prove how early anything was written. The time in each log entry is my own record. The same reviewer found a real bug, fixed in sello 0.1.2: signatures were stored by filename, so a later post with the same name could have broken an earlier seal. None of my seals were affected.
